<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Leuctra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/371-bce">371 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Leuctra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Leuctra was fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> in 371 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> and its Boeotian allies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> and a Spartan army under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleombrotus-i">Cleombrotus I</a>. Epaminondas massed his left wing 50 ranks deep, with the Sacred Band under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a>, and smashed the Spartan right, killing the king. The defeat ended the belief in Spartan invincibility and began a decade of Theban supremacy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra.jpg" alt="Battle of Leuctra" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edmund Ollier Publication date 1882 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pelopidas_leading_the_Thebans_at_the_battle_of_Leuctra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-antalcidas">King's Peace</a> of 386 BCE, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a>, guaranteed the autonomy of Greek cities not under Persian control. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> used that clause to force the Boeotian League, dominated by Thebes, to disband, and in 382 BCE the Spartan general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoebidas">Phoebidas</a> seized the Theban acropolis, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmea</a>. In late 379 BCE an anti-Spartan faction of Theban exiles overthrew the pro-Spartan oligarchs, and the Boeotian War began in 378 BCE.</p><p>In 371 BCE both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> were ready for peace. The Theban ambassadors wished to sign for the whole Boeotian League, but the Spartans demanded that each Boeotian city sign separately, and the Thebans refused. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> isolated, Sparta sent King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleombrotus-i">Cleombrotus I</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, where his army met a hastily repositioned Boeotian force at Leuctra, a village in the territory of Thespiae.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Ancient sources disagree on the numbers. The modern consensus is that Cleombrotus led about 10,000 Peloponnesian infantry, including 700 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartiates">Spartiates</a>, and 1,000 cavalry, against 7,000 Boeotian infantry and 700 cavalry under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a> commanding the 300-strong Sacred Band. Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanxes</a> were usually about eight ranks deep with the best troops on the right. Cleombrotus's was at most twelve deep with the Spartans on the right. Epaminondas, breaking with tradition, massed the Thebans at least fifty shields deep on the left, opposite the Spartans, and held back the other Boeotians in a shallower line.</p><p>The ancient accounts differ in detail, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>, the only contemporary, gives reasons for the Spartan defeat more than a narrative. He reports that the Spartan cavalry was beaten and fled through their own infantry just as the Thebans charged. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> instead has the Spartans opening out their right wing to envelop the Thebans, and the Sacred Band charging before the line was closed. The massed Theban column drove back the Spartan right, Cleombrotus was mortally wounded, and the Spartan left gave way when it saw the right pushed back. Xenophon records almost 1,000 Lacedaemonian dead, about 400 of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartiates">Spartiates</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A truce let both armies collect their dead, and the Thebans set up a victory trophy. More than a quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>'s full citizens had been lost, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> called the battle the single blow that ended Spartan dominance. Democratic factions revived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, and in the winter of 370/69 BCE a Theban-led army invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> and Messenia, freeing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a> of Messenia. Within a decade Sparta had lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a> and half its territory.</p><p>The years 371 to 362 BCE are known as the Theban hegemony, which ended at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mantinea-362-bce">Second Battle of Mantinea</a> in 362 BCE, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> died. Epaminondas's tactics are commonly thought to have influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>, who lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> as a hostage between 368 and 365 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Leuctra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the <b>Battle of Leuctra</b> in 371 BCE Epaminondas massed his left wing 50 ranks deep against the Spartan king Cleombrotus I, who was killed, shattering the reputation of Spartan arms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its fatal weakness was manpower: Herodotus put the citizen body at about 8,000 men in 480 BCE, but only about 700 Spartiates fought at the <b>Battle of Leuctra</b> in 371 BCE, and some 400 of them died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Trained and led by Epaminondas and Pelopidas, with the Sacred Band of Thebes, the Theban army won at <b>Leuctra</b> in 371 BCE, then carried its arms into the Peloponnesus, freeing many helots, and sent expeditions to Thessaly and Macedon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Their numbers fell steeply: Herodotus reckons about 8,000 men in 480 BCE; at <b>Leuctra</b> in 371 BCE about 400 of the 700 Spartiates present were killed; and Aristotle put them at fewer than a thousand.</blockquote><a href="/societies/sparta">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Sparta</a></li><li><blockquote>The Thebans and their Boeotian allies under Epaminondas defeated a Spartan army near <b>Leuctra</b> in Boeotia, killing King Cleombrotus I. The defeat ended the belief in Spartan invincibility and began a decade of Theban supremacy in Greece.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-6">6 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Sparta's defeat at <b>Leuctra</b> in 371 BCE freed the Arcadians to act together.</blockquote><a href="/state/arcadian-league">Arcadian League</a></li></ul></section>
